# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sub4 — repository hygiene
#
# The rule this file exists to enforce: the repository holds SOURCE, and
# nothing a build or a developer's local session produces. Before this file
# existed the repo tracked one developer's scheme-management plist while
# 31,488 lines of application source sat untracked — exactly backwards.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# macOS
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
Icon?

# Xcode — per-user state. Window positions, breakpoints, which scheme was last
# selected. Never shared; regenerated on demand.
xcuserdata/
*.xcuserstate
*.xcscmblueprint
*.xccheckout
*.perspectivev3
*.mode1v3
*.mode2v3
*.pbxuser

# Build products and derived data
build/
DerivedData/
*.o
*.hmap
*.ipa
*.dSYM
*.dSYM.zip
*.xcarchive
*.moved-aside

# Swift Package Manager
# Package.resolved is deliberately NOT ignored: it pins exact dependency
# versions, which is what makes a clean clone reproducible. When GRDB is added
# it must be committed.
.build/
.swiftpm/xcode/package.xcworkspace/xcuserdata/

# Playgrounds
timeline.xctimeline
playground.xcworkspace

# Secrets — belt and braces. Credentials belong in Keychain and must never
# reach a file in this tree.
*.p12
*.mobileprovision
secrets.plist
scripts/apply-*.py

# Patch delivery artefacts — patch 384.
#
# A patch zip drops a README and a commit message at the repository root; the
# workflow moves both to ~/Downloads before committing. When that step is
# skipped, `git add -A` tracks them — which is how `README-381.md` and
# `sub4-commit-381.txt` ended up in the tree on 16 August. Same reason
# `scripts/apply-*.py` is ignored above: it is delivery, not source.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash so `docs/` and `docs/context/README.md` are
# untouched, and `README.md` itself does not match the pattern.
/README-*.md
/sub4-commit-*.txt
